i_name":"","subject":"","author":"John Duke","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3382","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3382"}},{"id":"2861","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4165","description":"To see the full-sized version of this photo, <a href=\"http://www.americanwhitewater.org/photos/?photoid=2399\">click here</a>.","caption":"View from bridge at putin","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"RC Forney","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2861","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2861"}},{"id":"2944","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4489","description":"Mddletown gauge","caption":"Typical beaver dam and strainer","poi_name":"","subject":"Josh  Armadillo","author":"John Duke","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2944","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2944"}},{"id":"3381","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4486","description":"","caption":"Map of south half","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"John Duke","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3381","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3381"}},{"id":"3258","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4487","description":"","caption":"Teenie weenie gorge","poi_name":"","subject":"Josh Crewser,Jeff Brown","author":"John Duke","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3258","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3258"}},{"id":"3259","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4488","description":"very small rapid at the exit of the mini gorge,  pursay.  it could be pretty intense with another foot of water.","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"josh  crewser and  geoff","author":"jon duke","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3259","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3259"}},{"id":"1kY67HhtaE75ROj9OW60t","ext":"jpg","post_id":"z5Sm5XruatnzDBEKlyEz5","description":"Several sections of the creek are dark and well-shaded, and home to small groves of eastern hemlocks.","caption":"Catoctin Creek","poi_name":null,"subject":"Eastern hemlocks along the creek","author":"Jacob Malcom","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-03-03T20:02:08.000Z","updated_at":"2024-03-03T20:02:08.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1kY67HhtaE75ROj9OW60t","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1kY67HhtaE75ROj9OW60t"}},{"id":"JTTWbU0z15Y5GXFAJ6kVF","ext":"jpg","post_id":"z5Sm5XruatnzDBEKlyEz5","description":"Sycamore and other trees along the creek make for lovely views.","caption":"Catoctin Creek","poi_name":null,"subject":"A semi-random spot on Catoctin Creek","author":"Jacob Malcom","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-03-03T20:02:08.000Z","updated_at":"2024-03-03T20:02:08.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/JTTWbU0z15Y5GXFAJ6kVF","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/JTTWbU0z15Y5GXFAJ6kVF"}},{"id":"8IcvDgzFe4NbSkIRgU69G","ext":"jpg","post_id":"z5Sm5XruatnzDBEKlyEz5","description":"Nice little pebbly beach to set up the packraft and strap on the bike.","caption":"Launch spot - Pebble Beach, Catoctin Nature Park","poi_name":null,"subject":"Launch spot - Pebble Beach, Catoctin Nature Park","author":"Jacob Malcom","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-03-03T20:02:08.000Z","updated_at":"2024-03-03T20:02:08.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8IcvDgzFe4NbSkIRgU69G","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8IcvDgzFe4NbSkIRgU69G"}}],"detail":{"id":"714","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Ed Evangelidi testifies: Gertler has this same trip at roughly 25 miles. There is a gauge at Rte. 17 and the dam there and riffles below should have sufficient water to considering putting in there. Should be cleanly runnable without scraping to consider putting on further upstream. The park service is considering discontinuing access to the Potomac River takeout at Lander, but it is still open at this time.\n\n**access points from John Duke**\n\nany questions [e mail me!](mailto:dukejohn999@aol.com)\n\nMile 0.0--Rt. 40 bridge: not recommended, use the park on river right (Doub's Meadow Park) just downstream\n\nMile 1.3--Brethern Church road: room for 3 or 4 cars with a gravy put-in, this is a low-water bridge and must be carried\n\nmile 3.7--Rt. 17 bridge just south of myersville: not recommended, very little parking and farm fences on all sides.\n\nMile 6.7--Station road bridge: not recommended\n\nMile 6.8 to 7.1--Shank road follows on river right with nice access pull-offs.\n\nMile 9.8--Alt. Rt. 40 bridge: not recommended\n\nmile 11.3 -- bidle road bridge: not recommended\n\nMile 12.3--Rt. 17 bridge just south of middletown: excellent access just downstream of the bridge at the USGS. gauge\n\nMile 14.3--Bennies hill rd.: OK for a couple of cars\n\nMile 16.0--Sumantown road bridge: Park property on downstream river left with OK parking.\n\nMile 16.2--Catoctin Creek Park &amp; Nature Center park property on river right with parking a short walk from the creek.\n\nMile 18.7--Poffenberger road bridges and follows the creek for 1/2 mile but is posted!\n\nMile 20.8--Rt. 383 bridge: not recommended\n\nMile 22.4--Rt.180 bridge: nice parking on downstream left\n\nMile 24.7--Rt.464 bridge: Ausherman road hangs an upstream river right with decent access\n\nMile 28.4--Confluence with the MIGHTY Potomac river\n\nMile 28.8--C&amp;O canal at Lander: Excellent boat ramp on the Potomac\n\n**to locate access points see maps below.**\n\n**Camping areas**\n\n[Gambrill state park](http://www.dnr.state.md.us/publiclands/western/gambrill.html)\n\n[Greenbrier State Park](http://www.dnr.state.md.us/publiclands/western/greenbrier.html)\n\nBoth parks are conveniently located off Rt.40.One to the east of the creek and one to the west\n\n**Suggested river trips and descriptions**\n\n\\#1 Myersville Doub's Meadow Park to Shank Road---6.8 miles\n\nThis section contains the best and worst! At mile 2 the sounds of I-70,The sights of developments,and the smell of french fries deter you from this otherwise attractive stream. But don't worry below below the rt.17 bridge, this stream enters the'TEENIE WEENIE GORGE' with much better scenery and nice class 2- rapids on down to Shank road!\n\nThe low-water bridge at Brethern church is a mandatory portage!\n\nthere is also one tree carry above Brethern church road.\n\n\\#2 Shank road to MD rt.17 below middletown--5.5 miles\n\n\\#3 MD rt.17 to Catoctin Creek Park &amp; Nature Center--4 miles\n\n\\#4 Catoctin Creek Park &amp; Nature Center to Rt.180--6 miles\n\n\\#5 Rt.180 to Lander on the mighty Potomac--6.4 miles\n\nSections 2-5 are not quite as lively as section 1 but are still a nice way to spend a nice day on relatively unspoiled stream..In the fall of 2002 there were no mandatory tree carries but there were quite a few trees and brush to manuever around! Also there are many Beaver dams all with passage..","averageGradient":"12","maxGradient":null,"length":"26","correlations":[{"reachID":"714","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"2.2","beginMediumRunnable":"2.5","beginHighRunnable":"6","endHighRunnable":"6.8"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"CATOCTIN CREEK NEAR MIDDLETOWN, MD","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSour